      "content": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cassert>\n\nint main() {\n\tint n,s;\n\tstd::cin >> n >> s;\n\n\t// Flatten matrix into contiguous 1D array for better spatial locality\n\tstd::vector<int> mat(n * n);\n\tfor(int i=0;i<n;++i) {\n\t\tfor(int j=0;j<n;++j) {\n\t\t\tscanf(\"%d\", &mat[i * n + j]);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tassert(s>0 && s<n && n>0 && n<8000);\n\n\t// Compute initial sum of top-left SxS submatrix using row pointers for clarity\n\tint sum = 0;\n\tfor(int i=0;i<s;++i) {\n\t\tconst int* row = &mat[i * n];\n\t\tfor(int j=0;j<s;++j) {\n\t\t\tsum += row[j];\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tint mxSum = sum;\n\n\t// Sliding window with flat indexing; dir indicates horizontal movement direction\n\tint dir = 1;\n\tint i = 0;\n\tint j = 0;\n\twhile(i + s <= n) {\n\t\tif( (j+s == n && dir==1) || (j==0 && dir==-1) ) { \n\t\t\t// Move down: subtract exiting row, add entering row\n\t\t\tint exitingRowIdx = i * n;\n\t\t\tint enteringRowIdx = (i + s) * n;\n\t\t\tfor(int k=0;k<s;++k) {\n\t\t\t\tsum -= mat[exitingRowIdx + j + k];\n\t\t\t\tsum += mat[enteringRowIdx + j + k];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\ti++;\n\t\t\tdir *= -1;\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t// Move left or right: subtract exiting column, add entering column\n\t\t\tint exitingCol = (dir == 1) ? j : j + s - 1;\n\t\t\tint enteringCol = (dir == 1) ? j + s : j - 1;\n\t\t\tconst int* baseRow = &mat[i * n];\n\t\t\tfor(int k=0;k<s;++k) {\n\t\t\t\tsum -= baseRow[k * n + exitingCol];\n\t\t\t\tsum += baseRow[k * n + enteringCol];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tj += dir;\n\t\t}\n\t\tmxSum = std::max(mxSum, sum);\n\t}\n\n\tprintf(\"%d\\n\", mxSum);\n}\n",
      "path": "src/solution.cpp"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Flattening the matrix into a contiguous 1D array improves spatial locality by eliminating indirections from vector-of-vectors. This reduces cache misses during row-wise accesses in the sliding window algorithm. Additionally, using local pointers for rows enables better compiler optimization (auto-vectorization). The column updates remain strided but overall memory traffic is reduced.",
  "notes": "This transformation focuses on data layout and loop order. It does not change the algorithm complexity (still O(S N^2)), but improves constant factors through better cache utilization. All indices are verified to match original sliding window logic.",
